Let $x^\circ$ be the measure of the middle angle, $\angle B.$  Then $\angle A = (x - d)^\circ$ and $\angle C = (x + d)^\circ$ for some common difference $d.$  Since the angles of a triangle add up to $180^\circ,$
\[x - d + x + x + d = 180,\]so $x = 60.$

[asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

pair A, B, C, F;

B = (0,0);
C = (2,0);
A = intersectionpoint(arc(B,sqrt(7),0,180),arc(C,2*sqrt(7),0,180));
F = foot(B,A,C);

draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(B--F);

label("$A$", A, N);
label("$B$", B, SW);
label("$C$", C, SE);
label("$F$", F, S);
label("$x - d$", (0.8,0.2));
label("$x$", (1.8,0.2));
label("$x + d$", (A + C)/2, E);
[/asy]

Let $F$ be the foot of the altitude from $B$ to $\overline{AC}.$  Then
\[\sin (x + d)^\circ = \sin C = \frac{BF}{BC}\]and
\[\sin (x - d)^\circ = \sin A = \frac{BF}{AB}.\]Since $BF = AB - BC,$
\[\frac{\sin (x + d)^\circ}{\sin (x - d)^\circ} = \frac{BF/BC}{BF/AB} = \frac{AB}{BC} = \frac{BF + BC}{BC} = \frac{BF}{BC} + 1 = \sin (x + d)^\circ + 1.\]Then
\[\frac{\sin (x + d)^\circ}{\sin (x + d)^\circ + 1} = \sin (x - d)^\circ.\]By sum-to-product and double-angle,
\begin{align*}
\frac{\sin (x + d)^\circ}{\sin (x + d)^\circ + 1} &= \frac{\sin x^\circ \cos d^\circ + \cos x^\circ \sin d^\circ}{\sin x^\circ \cos d^\circ + \cos x^\circ \sin d^\circ + 1} \\
&= \frac{\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \cos d^\circ + \frac{1}{2} \sin d^\circ}{\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \cos d^\circ + \frac{1}{2} \sin d^\circ + 1} \\
&= \frac{\sqrt{3} \cos d^\circ + \sin d^\circ}{\sqrt{3} \cos d^\circ + \sin d^\circ + 2} \\
&= \sin x^\circ \cos d^\circ - \cos x^\circ \sin d^\circ \\
&= \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \cos d^\circ - \frac{1}{2} \sin d^\circ.
\end{align*}Then
\[\sqrt{3} \cos d^\circ + \sin d^\circ = \left( \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \cos d^\circ - \frac{1}{2} \sin d^\circ \right) (\sqrt{3} \cos d^\circ + \sin d^\circ + 2).\]This simplifies to $\cos d^\circ (\sqrt{3} \cos d^\circ + \sin d^\circ + 1) = 0.$  Hence, $\cos d^\circ = 0$ or $\sqrt{3} \cos d^\circ + \sin d^\circ + 1 = 0.$

If $\cos d^\circ = 0,$ then $d = 90$ or $d = -90.$  But these lead to degenerate triangles, so we can rule these out.

If $\sqrt{3} \cos d^\circ + \sin d^\circ + 1 = 0,$ then
\[\sin d^\circ + \sqrt{3} \cos d^\circ = -1.\]Then
\[2 \sin \left( d^\circ + 60^\circ \right) = -1,\]so $\sin \left( d^\circ + 60^\circ \right) = -\frac{1}{2}.$  Hence, $d^\circ + 60^\circ = -30^\circ$ or $d^\circ + 60^\circ = 210^\circ.$  This leads to $d = -90$ or $d = 150.$  Again, $d = -90$ leads to a degenerate triangle, so $d = 150.$  Then
\[\sin \frac{C - A}{2} = \sin 75^\circ = \boxed{\frac{\sqrt{2} + \sqrt{6}}{4}}.\]TERMINATE

